<para id="RM00000160F00NX" category="C" type-id="3031K" name-id="0659S-00" from="200612" to="200912">
<dtccode>P0031/21</dtccode>
<dtcname>O2 (A/F) Sensor Heater Performance DeteriorationB1S1</dtcname>
<dtccode>P0032/21</dtccode>
<dtcname>O2 (A/F) Sensor Heater OvercurrentB1S1</dtcname>
<subpara id="RM00000160F00NX_01" type-id="31" category="03" proc-id="RM0550J___00002TU00000">
<name>Circuit Description</name>
<content5 releasenbr="1">
<list1 type="nonmark">
<item>
<ptxt>A three-way catalytic converter is used to efficiently purify the CO, HC and NOx in the exhaust gas. This three-way catalytic converter functions most efficiently near the stoichiometric air-fuel ratio. For this reason, CO, HC and NOx cannot be efficiently purified unless the engine is accurately controlled near the stoichiometric air-fuel ratio. The air fuel ratio sensor (A/F sensor) is installed for the purpose of this control. The air fuel ratio sensor (A/F sensor) outputs a voltage proportional to the air-fuel ratio, and the engine control computer controls the air-fuel ratio based on that voltage. The heated air fuel ratio sensor (A/F sensor) has a heater to warm the zirconium element. The heater is controlled by the engine control computer, and current flows to the heater to prevent the air fuel ratio sensor (A/F sensor) from cooling and becoming unable to operate accurately when the exhaust gas temperature is low (such as when intake air volume is low).</ptxt>
<figure>
<graphic graphicname="A088467J05" width="7.106578999in" height="2.775699831in"/>
</figure>
</item>
</list1>
<table frame="all" colsep="1" rowsep="1">
<tgroup cols="3" colsep="1" rowsep="1">
